module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.gate_closure_narrower_than_the_closure_its_merge_governs

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data gate_closure_narrower_than_the_closure_its_merge_governs: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "gate_closure_narrower_than_the_closure_its_merge_governs" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a change is admitted in repository A on the only gate empowered to refuse it, while the gate that compiles the closure the merge actually governs sits in repository B and cannot refuse anything in A.** INVALID STATE: NO GATE ELIGIBLE TO BLOCK A'S MERGE COMPILES BOTH REPOSITORIES. State it that way rather than as \"no gate compiles both\", because the second is false here and is the weaker claim: B's gate compiles A+B and has exactly the right closure. What is missing is not the closure. It is the AUTHORITY to refuse a merge in A, and a TRIGGER that fires on A's changes.",

    "THE MECHANISM IS A SPLIT BETWEEN TWO PROPERTIES THAT A GATE NORMALLY HOLDS TOGETHER. A gate is useful only when it both (i) compiles the closure a merge governs and (ii) can refuse that merge. In a one-repository lane those are the same gate and the split is invisible. Under a one-directional build-time coupling -- B builds against A's main -- they come apart: A's gate can refuse A's merges but compiles A alone, and B's gate compiles A+B but fires only on changes authored in B and can refuse only merges in B. The closure that would catch the incompatibility and the authority that could stop it exist, and are in different repositories.",

    "SPECIMEN, 2026-09-11, COMPLETE. Public gunbc#11057, \"Refuse a zero-field construction of a fields-declaring type\", landed green on public CI. It made a bare `ExitFailure` fail to RESOLVE -- `std.process` declares `ExitFailure { code, reason }`, and refusing the zero-field construction of a fields-declaring type is exactly what that PR was for, so the public change was correct and its gate was right to admit it. ELEVEN witnesses in gunbc-private used bare `ExitFailure` as their failure arm: `asset_lifecycle`, `buyers_pipeline`, `ci_sellable_unit`, `mobo_x_architecture`, `mobo_x_memory_census`, `panel_ruling_procedure`, `pareto_comparison_procedure`, `pricing_decomposition`, `rack_power_claims`, `site_power_survey`, `workflow_stage_gate`. gunbc-private CI builds against public main, so `private_roster_main` stopped resolving ENTIRELY -- 0 PASS, not a partial failure -- and every private PR plus private main went red at once.",

    "THE REPAIR, WITH ITS GENEALOGY, BECAUSE THE GENEALOGY IS ITSELF A FACT ABOUT THIS CLASS. Repaired by gunbc-private#81 (1b7f87e88f), merged 2026-09-11T20:55:06Z; gunbc-private#82 independently carried the same repair and closed unmerged at 21:03:18Z as superseded. TWO LANES INDEPENDENTLY BUILT THE SAME REPAIR, eight minutes apart, neither aware of the other. That is the predictable second-order cost of a break whose cause is invisible from the damaged repository: with no signal naming the origin, every observer starts from the symptom, and duplicated repair effort is the default rather than the exception. This row was first authored citing #82 as the repair -- an unmerged branch named as the event that restored main, inside an authority about merges -- and the correction is kept here rather than silently swapped, because the mis-citation was produced by the same missing-provenance condition the class describes.",

    "THE DETECTION SHAPE IS PART OF THE CLASS AND IS WHY THE FIRST READING WAS WRONG. The break was TOTAL rather than partial -- a module every witness resolves through stopped resolving, so the count went to zero rather than down -- and a zero reads as INFRASTRUCTURE, not as semantics. The first plausible hypothesis was \"private CI is broken\", which is a hypothesis about the runner, the checkout or the binary; the true reading, \"a public change removed a construction we depend on\", is not reachable from anything in B's own history. B's authors bisect against a change they did not make and cannot see. A PARTIAL failure would have pointed at the eleven sites and named the construction; totality is what destroys the pointer.",

    "HARM: the two signals are asymmetrically wrong in opposite directions. A's authors get NO signal at all -- their gate is green and stays green, so the cost they caused is invisible to them and never ranks (DESIGN section 5, externalization: a cost-causing actor leaving a caused cost unpriced onto another principal). B's authors get a signal that names none of the cause: a total red whose located diagnostics are all in B, about declarations B did not change, caused by a commit in a repository B's history does not contain. Every author-hour spent is spent by the side that did nothing, and, per the genealogy above, is spent more than once.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE, STATED OVER THE RIGHT PAIR. Do not ask \"does any gate compile both repositories\" -- that question returns yes here and clears a live defect. Ask, of each gate: what closure does it COMPILE, and which repositories' merges can it REFUSE? Take the union of the second over all gates that compile a given closure, and compare it with the set of repositories whose merges that closure governs. A gap is this class. The cheap field tell is directional: a repository that pins ANOTHER repository's branch (not a release, a branch) while the upstream's merge path names no gate that compiles the dependent.",

    "DISTINCT FROM THE SAME-REPO JOINTLY-INCOMPATIBLE CLASS, which is `gunbc.plans.ci_merge_freshness` (the class and its ceiling; gunbc#10984 recorded instance 1) and `gunbc.plans.joint_incompatibility_claim_join` (gunbc#11044, which scopes a mitigation beneath that ceiling). There, TWO changes in ONE repository are each green and their union compiles nowhere; the deficit is FRESHNESS -- each run compiled the right closure at the wrong time -- and a merge queue is available precisely because ONE gate both compiles the union and refuses the merge, which is why `gunbc.merge_admission` `receipt_is_admissible` can be the landed ceiling. Here ONE change in one repository breaks a DIFFERENT repository, and the deficit is a SPLIT between closure and authority that a queue does not address: queueing A's merges orders them against each other and still never consults the gate that compiles B. #11044's join is over ONE declaration index and does not reach across the repository boundary.",

    "DISTINCT FROM A BREAKING-CHANGE-TO-A-PUBLISHED-API CLASS. Nothing here is versioned or published. B pins A's MAIN BRANCH, so there is no release boundary that was skipped, no compatibility promise that was broken, and no deprecation window that would have helped: a window is a span between two published versions, and the coupling has neither endpoint. A repair framed as \"version the public surface\" answers a different class and would leave this one intact at any cadence.",

    "DISTINCT FROM THE STALE-MERGE-BASE CLASS (`verdict_stale_at_the_merge_instant`). Both sides were CURRENT: the public gate compiled current public main, and private CI built against public main as of its own run. Nothing was stale and no verdict was about a superseded tree. The union was never compiled by anything ELIGIBLE TO REFUSE THE MERGE, at any freshness.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. The failure occurs, is discovered by B's own CI after the fact, and is repaired by hand -- eleven sites, mechanically, and twice over. Nothing contained it; what contained the harm was that the repair was mechanical.",

    "CEILING: MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE, and the argument is materially cheaper than it looks once the invalid state is stated correctly. The question is decidable: whether A's change breaks B is answered by compiling A+B, a finite compilation of a closure both repositories already name. And that compilation ALREADY EXISTS AND ALREADY RUNS -- it is B's gate. So the repair is not \"build a second full compile of B inside A's gate\", which is what \"no gate compiles both\" would imply and which is the expensive reading; it is to BIND an existing closure to A's merge admission, by triggering it on A's changes and giving its verdict authority to refuse. The residual cost is real but is runner time on an existing job, not a new one. Rung 3 is still NOT claimed, and the reason is not cost: the invalid state stays WRITABLE. The binding is enrollment -- a dependent nobody enrolled, a workflow edit, a skipped or non-required job -- so safety depends on that binding executing and staying enrolled, which is the definition of rung 2 (DESIGN section 4b). Naming a cost as the ceiling would be the section 4b(1) inflation this row exists to avoid, and so would naming an enrollment as construction.",

    "NEXT TRIGGER, NAMED AS THE CAPABILITY AND NOT AS AN ARTIFACT: a gate whose verdict can REFUSE A MERGE IN THIS REPOSITORY, whose compiled closure includes EVERY repository that builds against this one, with the dependent set DISCOVERED rather than hand-listed -- sufficient to refuse a merge no gate empowered to block it has compiled against a dependent. All three conjuncts are load-bearing and each is a separate way to be satisfied while the class stays live. Drop the refusal authority and you have today's state exactly: the right closure compiles and reports, and the merge lands anyway. Drop the discovery and one workflow edit retires the row while a third dependent repository stays invisible. Drop the closure coverage and the gate blocks on a subset it happens to know. The grain test: the loss is over a SET of dependents and over the merge path of a repository, so a trigger phrased as \"add gunbc-private's suite to the public workflow\" is strictly narrower on two of the three axes at once.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
